\section{An Edge Is a Use, Not a Copy}

In a basic block, draw a directed edge from each definition to the operation that uses it. Two outgoing edges from one definition do not imply two evaluations. They mean that two operands refer to the same value. An expression tree cannot represent this fact without an extra convention: either it names the value at a leaf or it reproduces the computation at each use. A program DAG retains the identity directly. A pattern DAG extends the other side of the comparison: one instruction can implement several related operations and name more than one result. It need not have one result at the root of a tree. Blindell introduces both generalizations before analyzing their effect on matching and selection \cite[chapter~4]{blindell2016instruction}.

A useful match record distinguishes the operations owned by the instruction, the operands it still needs, and the values it makes available outside the instruction. In particular, ``owns the operation that computes $v$'' does not imply ``provides $v$ to other instructions.'' A target may compute an intermediate privately, with no way for another instruction to read it. Conversely, two matches can take the same value as an input without conflicting. A cover must supply those uses from a live-in or from a selected compatible definition. Any permission to recompute a value is part of the model, not a consequence of representing it with a DAG.

\section{Finding Matches Is Not Choosing Them}

Matching asks where a target pattern can be instantiated: compare opcodes and types, check operand positions, and honor identity constraints where a pattern node is reached by two paths. One route in Blindell's survey splits a pattern DAG into trees, matches the parts, then joins partial maps that agree on shared nodes. Another route directly searches for subgraph embeddings. General matching can have hard instances, but a fixed small pattern need not pose a hard search. For example, after choosing the root of an ordered pattern with fixed operand paths, a matcher can follow those paths and test equality where they meet. General bounds for matching do not imply that each root test is expensive.

Selection takes those instantiated candidates and decides which can coexist. For trees and an additive grammar, the usual dynamic program combines independently priced frontier subtrees. On a DAG, a frontier may be used several times: its producer choice can affect every consumer. Blindell presents the Koes--Goldstein reduction from SAT, using Boolean variable and operator nodes, auxiliary boxes, and a stop requirement. The unit-cost threshold in that construction distinguishes a consistent assignment from a cover that uses extra patterns. The key point is that the reduction creates a selection problem even with known candidate patterns; a faster matcher would not solve the consistency problem.

Strictly, it is the budget decision problem---whether a legal cover costs at most $B$---that is NP-complete in the general formulation. For an explicit polynomial-size candidate list and polynomially checkable legality, a selected list is a certificate: check coverage and operand requirements, then sum the costs. Computing a least-cost cover is NP-hard; it is a search and optimization task, not a yes-or-no language. A polynomial-time optimizer would answer the budget question by comparing its optimum with $B$.

