\section{A Budget Enforces Shared Operand Choices}

Consider a different, small SAT encoding for a hypothetical heterogeneous target. Each variable has a required definition that can produce the same numeric value in class \texttt{R} or \texttt{L}, for one cost unit either way. Exactly one form is selected per definition. For each three-literal clause create a required three-input operation, for example a wrapping sum of the three operand values. The literal signs are attributes of the IR opcode used to select eligible instruction forms; they do not change the sum. Each fast form implements the sum at cost one and restricts just one input slot to \texttt{R} for a positive literal, or \texttt{L} for a negative one. Its other slots accept either class. A fallback costs two and accepts all combinations. The target has no conversions in this model, and neither class has a capacity limit. Interpret \texttt{R} as true and \texttt{L} as false, but note that these are classes, not Boolean values computed at runtime. The matcher records the fast candidates with their class requirements; it does not test a class assignment that has yet to be made.

\begin{verbatim}
C1: p or !q or r
C2: !p or q or !r
C3: p or q or !r

p=R q=L r=R    # Each definition is paid for once.
C1: fast(p)   C2: fallback   C3: fast(p)   total 7
p=R q=R r=R    # Changing q changes all its uses.
C1: fast(p)   C2: fast(q)    C3: fast(p)   total 6
\end{verbatim}

\Cref{fig:visual-270} singles out the class constraint that changes at \texttt{C2}. The class of \(q\) is chosen once for the whole cover, even when a fast form constrains a different operand.

