Three definitions and three clause operations cost at least six, so the second line of choices reaches the lower bound. The first choice cannot improve to six without changing a producer: every fast form at \texttt{C2} rejects its chosen input class. A selector cannot make \texttt{q} appear in \texttt{R} at that use and \texttt{L} elsewhere. For the general construction with $n$ variables and $m$ three-literal clauses, there are two candidates per definition and four per clause. These lists are obtained directly from the formula, with no graph-embedding search; the sign attributes have only eight possibilities. At budget $n+m$ all clauses must take a fast form, each witnessing a satisfied literal. Conversely a satisfying assignment provides a valid choice of class at each definition and a fast form at every clause. Thus this restricted selection model has an NP-complete budget decision problem even with singleton matches. The hardness comes from a jointly chosen interface, not from matching a large pattern.
